1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:02,583
Here is a strange thing.

2
00:00:02,583 --> 00:00:11,094
For the next four episodes, the ground gives us very little: a few post-holes, some plain pottery, no cities, no palaces.

3
00:00:12,767 --> 00:00:18,585
And yet we know more about what these people thought than about almost anyone else in the ancient world.

4
00:00:18,585 --> 00:00:25,147
Because they left something else entirely — a book, carried in memory for centuries before anyone wrote it down.

5
00:00:27,833 --> 00:00:29,079
So the sources flip.

6
00:00:29,079 --> 00:00:33,882
For the first time, texts do most of the work, and archaeology only checks them.

7
00:00:33,882 --> 00:00:37,796
That is a real limitation, and it shapes everything that follows:

8
00:00:37,796 --> 00:00:44,620
we hear these people describe themselves, and we have very little independent evidence to test what they say.

9
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:55,095
The Rigveda is the oldest surviving text composed in India, a collection of more than a thousand hymns in ten books.

10
00:00:57,067 --> 00:01:01,259
It was composed in an archaic form of Sanskrit, and then carried orally,

11
00:01:01,259 --> 00:01:08,036
by families of reciters trained to repeat it exactly, syllable by syllable, for many centuries before it was written.

12
00:01:08,036 --> 00:01:12,413
That is why its language is older than the manuscripts it survives in.

13
00:01:14,733 --> 00:01:17,352
The world of the Rigveda is a river world.

14
00:01:17,352 --> 00:01:24,294
It calls itself the land of seven rivers, Sapta Sindhu, and the rivers it names most often lie in the north-west.

15
00:01:24,294 --> 00:01:26,787
The Sindhu is mentioned constantly.

16
00:01:29,633 --> 00:01:32,684
The Saraswati is praised as the greatest of rivers.

17
00:01:32,684 --> 00:01:34,386
The Ganga appears only once.

18
00:01:34,386 --> 00:01:40,670
That pattern tells you roughly where these people were, and in which direction they had not yet gone.

19
00:01:43,300 --> 00:01:44,774
Their economy was pastoral.

20
00:01:44,774 --> 00:01:51,709
Cattle were the measure of wealth, so completely that the word for a battle in the Rigveda literally means a search for cows.

21
00:01:55,367 --> 00:02:00,002
They farmed too, mainly barley, but land was not the thing people counted.

22
00:02:00,002 --> 00:02:01,105
Cattle were.

23
00:02:03,333 --> 00:02:05,764
And the politics were tribal, not royal.

24
00:02:05,764 --> 00:02:07,898
People belonged to a jana, a tribe.

25
00:02:07,898 --> 00:02:10,565
Its leader was a raja — but this is a chief,

26
00:02:10,565 --> 00:02:16,793
chosen for leadership in war and for protecting cattle, not a king with a territory and a treasury.

27
00:02:22,233 --> 00:02:29,124
He was advised, and checked, by assemblies: the sabha, a smaller body, and the samiti, a larger one.

28
00:02:29,124 --> 00:02:33,354
He took bali, offerings, but these were voluntary, not taxes.

29
00:02:33,354 --> 00:02:39,358
The gods they invoked — Indra, Agni, Varuna — were worshipped at the fire, in the open.

30
00:02:39,358 --> 00:02:42,183
There were no temples and no images.

31
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:51,305
Now the question everyone asks, and the one where opinion runs hottest.

32
00:02:51,305 --> 00:02:53,577
Where did the Vedic people come from?

33
00:02:53,577 --> 00:02:55,071
One body of scholarship,

34
00:02:55,071 --> 00:03:00,632
resting mainly on language — on how Sanskrit relates to other Indo-European languages — argu

35
00:03:00,632 --> 00:03:06,552
es that Indo-Aryan speakers moved into the subcontinent from Central Asia over a long period.

36
00:03:08,467 --> 00:03:11,819
Another argues for development within the subcontinent,

37
00:03:11,819 --> 00:03:17,447
pointing to continuity in the material record and to the absence of any evidence of invasion.

38
00:03:17,447 --> 00:03:19,422
Two things are worth separating.

39
00:03:19,422 --> 00:03:25,050
Almost all scholars now reject the old idea of a violent invasion destroying Harappan cities.

40
00:03:25,050 --> 00:03:29,480
The narrower question of migration is genuinely open and actively argued.

41
00:03:29,480 --> 00:03:32,953
This series states both positions and takes neither.

42
00:03:38,933 --> 00:03:42,416
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

43
00:03:42,416 --> 00:03:44,710
Early Vedic in four words.

44
00:03:44,710 --> 00:03:46,154
Cows, not crops.

45
00:03:46,154 --> 00:03:47,899
Chief, not king.

46
00:03:52,567 --> 00:03:53,284
Dig Quiz.

47
00:03:53,284 --> 00:03:54,647
Pause and name it.

48
00:03:54,647 --> 00:04:02,119
First: a collection of more than a thousand hymns, in ten books, the oldest text composed in India.

49
00:04:04,533 --> 00:04:05,395
The Rigveda.

50
00:04:05,395 --> 00:04:09,571
Second: the two assemblies that advised and checked the chief.

51
00:04:09,571 --> 00:04:11,594
The sabha and the samiti.

52
00:04:13,667 --> 00:04:18,314
Third: the land of seven rivers, where most of the Rigveda is set.

53
00:04:18,314 --> 00:04:19,284
Sapta Sindhu.

54
00:04:19,284 --> 00:04:24,509
In Mains, a standard question asks you to describe Early Vedic polity.

55
00:04:27,100 --> 00:04:34,322
The marks are in the word chief: no territory, no standing army, no regular tax, and assemblies that mattered.

56
00:04:36,900 --> 00:04:44,297
A page of the Rigveda takes its place in the gallery: the oldest thing in this museum that is not an object at all, but a memory.

57
00:04:47,033 --> 00:04:51,995
Next gallery: iron, the move east, and the moment a chief becomes a king.

58
00:04:51,995 --> 00:04:56,118
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

